---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Carl Edwards is a product engineering leader whose career spans Pandora, ServiceChannel, Human Interest, and Zero. He builds teams, ships products, and stays close to customers and code.">
  <section class="container-prose pt-12 pb-20">
    <div class="text-mono-tag mb-4">About</div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold text-ink-100 tracking-tight">
      Hi, I'm Carl.
    </h1>

    <div class="prose prose-invert prose-lg mt-10 max-w-none">
      <p class="lead">
        I build engineering teams and help them turn complicated problems into
        products people can rely on. My career has taken me from debugging database
        software to engineering leadership at Pandora, ServiceChannel, Human Interest,
        and Zero. Throughout it, I've stayed close to the customers, the people doing
        the work, and the code.
      </p>

      <h2>From building software to building teams</h2>
      <p>
        Early in my career at Sybase, supporting database client products taught me
        to follow a problem wherever it led. A customer's experience could depend on
        several systems, platforms, and teams. Understanding those connections—and
        taking responsibility for the outcome—became the foundation of how I work.
      </p>
      <p>
        I spent thirteen years at Pandora, progressing from senior software engineer
        through device engineering management and into a VP of Engineering role.
        The work brought together connected devices, automotive, mobile, advertising,
        and partners. As my responsibilities grew, so did the importance of helping
        people make decisions together: understanding competing needs, making
        tradeoffs clear, and giving teams a direction they could act on.
      </p>
      <p>
        At ServiceChannel, I built a West Coast engineering and program-management
        organization and brought mobile development in-house. At Human Interest,
        leading engineering in financial services deepened my appreciation for
        correctness, reliability, and the operational work behind the customer
        experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most recently, as VP of Engineering at Zero, I set technical direction and
        wrote production code alongside a small team. Our work included AI-powered
        nutrition analysis and guidance, improvements to the mobile and backend
        experience, and making production quality more visible so engineers could
        act on it.
      </p>

      <h2>What I bring to a team</h2>
      <p>
        I'm useful where customer needs, technical complexity, and organizational
        constraints meet. I listen, connect the pieces, and help turn an unclear
        situation into a next step. Sometimes that means coaching a manager or
        resolving a disagreement. Sometimes it means making an architectural
        decision, coordinating an incident, or writing the code.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hands-on leadership means staying close enough to contribute without
        becoming the bottleneck. I want people to develop ownership, receive clear
        feedback, and know that they can raise a concern or admit uncertainty.
        High standards and respect for people belong together.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also care about what happens after we ship. Do customers understand the
        change? Does it help them? What do usage, reliability, and feedback tell us?
        Those questions guide what we build next and where we invest the team's time.
      </p>

      <h2>Still curious. Still building.</h2>
      <p>
        I learned by building and debugging, starting with BASIC and 6502 assembly.
        That curiosity is still with me. Today I'm exploring practical AI tools and
        workflows, building developer tools, and creating interactive lessons that
        make computing easier to understand. You can explore my
        <a href="/projects/">projects</a>, try the <a href="/learn/">lessons</a>, or
        read my <a href="/blog/">notes on building and leading</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        One principle has stayed with me since those early Sybase days:
        <a href="/blog/leave-the-product-better-than-you-found-it/">leave the product
        better than you found it</a>. It applies to the software and to the team
        doing the work.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Let's talk</h2>
      <p>
        I'm looking for a role where I can stay close to users, shape what gets
        built, and help a team do work it is proud of. If that sounds like what
        you're building, I'd enjoy a conversation. Reach me on
        <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/the-carl-edwards">LinkedIn</a> or use the
        <strong>Start a conversation</strong> button at the top of the page.
      </p>

      <h2 id="elsewhere">Elsewhere</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/carledwards">GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://linkedin.com/in/the-carl-edwards">LinkedIn</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://twitter.com/the_CarlEdwards">Twitter / X</a></li>
        <li><a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-ink-400 text-sm mt-12">
        This site is built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> and
        deployed on Cloudflare Pages. Source on
        <a href="https://github.com/carledwards/thecarledwards-site">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
